================================================================================
0. METRIC CONTRACT VALIDATION
================================================================================
The ontology enforces a METRIC CONTRACT pattern for On-Time Delivery:

  Canonical Definition: "Percentage of eligible deliveries completed on or
  before the applicable committed date."

  This is ONE metric with ONE definition. The numbers differ across personas
  because the committed date differs -- not because the metric is inconsistent.

  Lens Routing:
    Supplier (Procurement): receipt_date <= promised_date        -> 86.61%
    Carrier (Logistics):    actual_delivery <= estimated_delivery -> 47.52%
    Customer (Planning):    shipped_date <= request_date + 5 days
                            AND shipped_qty >= ordered_qty        ->  5.39%
    Enterprise (Executive): Weighted average across all 3         -> 39.50%

  Why do the numbers differ?
    Supplier OTD is high (86.61%) because supplier lead times are well-managed.
    Carrier OTD is lower (47.52%) because ship/delivery dates are independently
    randomized in synthetic data, creating realistic transit variance.
    Customer OTIF is very low (5.39%) because fulfillment lead time averages
    13.6 days vs a 5-day SLA grace window AND requires in-full quantity.
    This is intentional: it demonstrates the ontology correctly distinguishes
    OTD (timing only) from OTIF (timing + quantity).

